  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways near-infrared (NIR) light can trigger useful responses in soft materials for biomedical use. Overall, it shows that photothermal heating from NIR changes polymer networks or nanomaterials, causing shape change, self-healing, drug release, or direct tumor killing.\n\nAt the top, elongated hydrogel-like strips are shown before and after NIR exposure. In one case the internal polymer chains appear to reorganize and the strip changes dimensions; in another, a layered structure bends into an arc, suggesting light-driven actuation from unequal contraction or expansion through the thickness.\n\nThe middle schematic presents a cracked gel disk that is exposed to NIR and then heals. Enlarged network views indicate that heating activates reversible, dynamic bonds, allowing the polymer chains to reconnect across the damaged interface and restore the material.\n\nThe bottom part focuses on cancer treatment. A cluster of live cancer cells contains NIR-responsive nanostructures. If these carriers contain drug, NIR causes drug release and cell death. Even without drug, the same light-generated heat produces a localized hot region that kills tumor cells through photothermal therapy. A small legend distinguishes live cells, dead cells, and drug molecules.",
